{
  "$comment": "Known AI crawlers and AI agents that the Worker counts (src/ai-bots.ts, table ai_bot_visits). `token` is matched case-insensitively as a substring of the User-Agent header; the first entry that matches wins, so put more specific tokens first. `match: false` = a robots.txt control token only (the operator never sends it as a User-Agent), listed for completeness and never counted. purpose: training = collects pages to train models; search = builds an AI search index; user = fetches a page because a person asked an assistant right now; mixed = several of these. Names come from the User-Agent header, which anyone can send; since 2026-10-18 each visit also stores Cloudflare's own verdict (request.cf.verifiedBotCategory): verified / not_verified / unknown. `cloudflare_radar` = the bot's slug in Cloudflare's verified bots and agents directory (https://radar.cloudflare.com/bots/directory/<slug>, checked 2026-10-18 via the Radar API GET /radar/bots), or null when Cloudflare does not list it: for a null bot every visit shows not_verified even when it is genuine, so only a listed bot's not_verified visits suggest a fake name. test/ai-bots.test.ts checks this file (unique names and tokens, every robots.txt AI agent listed, sample User-Agents). `evidence` says how sure we are that the token is real: 'operator docs' = the operator publishes it; anything else names the public source (e.g. observed in UA databases). Not added because no operator or well-sourced public token exists (checked 2026-10-18): xAI/Grok, Alibaba/Qwen, DeepSeek (third-party lists name GrokBot, xAI-SearchBot, QwenBot, DeepSeekBot, but the operators publish none and they are not observed reliably); OpenAI 'ChatGPT agent' (signs requests with Web Bot Auth, no own UA token on https://platform.openai.com/docs/bots); 'GoogleAgent-*' (Google publishes only Google-Agent).",
